  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places high over Manitoba stretching\nsouthward into the Dakotas, while low remains over the lower\nMidwest. Upper level analysis places ridge over the Great Basin\ninto the Northern Rockies, with north-northwest flow over the\nnorthern plains. Over our area, low clouds cover northwest into\nmuch of central North Dakota, with some persistant fog \ncontinuing to affect parts of north central/northwest North \nDakota generally around the Minot area, though this has been \nimproving over the past hour or two.\n\nFor tonight, cloud cover will linger over much of the area, but\nto the west guidance continues to indicate fog development later\nthis evening, remaining through the overnight hours. \n\nOn Thursday, with the exception of the fog lingering into the\nmorning over the west, a quiet day is on tap with the ridge to\nthe west keeping us in northwesterly flow. \n\nRidge pushes to the east as we get into the weekend and early\nnext week which will keep dry weather in place, but will result\nin a gradual warming trend. By the start of the next work week,\nhigh temperatures around 10 to 15 degrees above average will be\npossible. As we close in on mid-week, a Pacific trough will push\neasterly, bringing rain chances to the forecast.",
